WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.280
"How Much Cancer Does Processed Meat Cause?"

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:11.360
In 2018, arguably the most
prestigious cancer research institution

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:14.800
in the world, the International Agency
for Research on Cancer (IARC),

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:17.600
within the World Health Organization,
published their report

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:21.120
on processed meat, concluding
that foods like bacon, ham,

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:24.800
hot dogs, lunch meat, and
sausages are cancer causing,

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:27.920
classifying processed meat
as a Group 1 carcinogen.

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:30.320
“These findings,” concluded
the director of the agency,

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:32.720
“further support current
public health recommendations

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:35.520
to limit the intake of meat.”

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:38.240
Critics questioned putting
processed meat in the same

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:42.160
carcinogenic classification
as asbestos or tobacco.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:45.840
Or as a pesticide company
roughly put it, how can eating

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:50.720
processed meat fall into the
same category as mustard gas.

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:53.520
The classifications only relate
to the strength of evidence

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:57.760
that the agent causes cancer
or not, not how much cancer.

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:00.160
This does not mean that
they are all equally dangerous.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:03.680
It’s safer to eat a sandwich
filled with pastrami than plutonium,

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:06.160
even though they are both
Group 1 carcinogens;

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:09.680
both substances known to
cause cancer in people.

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:13.840
Okay, so just how
dangerous is meat?

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:17.440
The relative risk of colorectal
cancer was 18 percent

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:22.160
for every 50 grams a day. Okay,
so what exactly does that mean?

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:26.080
Well, 50 grams is about one hot
dog, or two breakfast links,

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:28.480
or two slices of
Canadian bacon or ham.

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:31.120
So, a daily sandwich with one
or two slices of baloney

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:34.400
would increase your colorectal
cancer risk by 18 percent.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.640
But a half-pound
pastrami on rye would

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:39.360
bump it up more like 80 percent.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:44.080
Okay, but what does the 18 percent
increased risk really mean?

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:48.080
One way to look at it is
absolute risk versus relative risk.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:51.120
Assuming that the lifetime
risk of colorectal cancer is

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:54.640
about 5 percent (1 in 20),
increasing your risk about

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:57.040
20 percent would only
bump up your absolute risk

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:01.600
of getting colorectal cancer
from 5 percent to 6 percent.

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:04.480
Now, on a population scale,
an 18 percent drop in risk

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:07.680
could mean about 20,000 fewer
cases of colorectal cancer

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:10.640
every year in the United States,
20,000 fewer families a year

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:14.080
dealing with that diagnosis,
if we swapped out the daily

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:18.080
bologna sandwich for hummus or
chose veggie dogs instead.

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:21.760
So, it all depends on
how you look at it.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:25.200
Colorectal cancer is our second
leading cause of cancer death

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:28.240
for men and women combined,
after lung cancer.

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:31.120
So, if you don’t smoke,
colon and rectal cancer

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:33.920
may be your greatest cancer nemesis.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:37.440
But we can drop the risk
of getting it by about a fifth

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:40.640
with a single dietary tweak:
cutting a serving of

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:43.360
processed meat out of our daily diet.

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:46.160
How does 18 percent
increased cancer risk

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:48.560
compare to other risky behavior?

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:52.000
In my testimony before the Dietary
Guidelines Scientific Committee,

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:55.440
I made what may sound
like a hyperbolic metaphor.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:58.080
I asked, “We try not to
smoke around our kids,

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:02.880
why would we send them to
school with a baloney sandwich?”

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:04.160
That is not hyperbole.

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:06.960
According to the Surgeon
General, living with a smoker

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:10.320
increases your risk of
lung cancer by 15 percent.

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:13.440
So, breathing second-hand
smoke day in and day out

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:16.880
increases your risk of lung
cancer almost as much as

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:19.840
eating a serving of processed
meat day in and day out

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:22.880
increases your risk
of colorectal cancer.

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:25.760
The meat industry responded
by saying that the risks

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:28.560
and benefits must be
considered together before

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:30.480
telling people what to eat or breathe.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:33.200
Think about all the baloney benefits.

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:37.360
Lunch meat isn’t just about
cancer but convenience.

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:40.080
Indeed, processed meat
isn’t just about cancer.

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:42.720
An article railing against
the World Health Organization’s

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:46.400
“meat terrorism” cited the
Global Burden of Disease studies

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:49.200
comparing how many cancer deaths
are caused by processed meat

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:52.560
consumption compared to
tobacco or alcohol use.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:54.480
But if you look at the
study they’re referencing,

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:58.080
the 30-something thousand
deaths attributable to higher

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:01.600
processed meat intake are
just the colorectal cancer deaths,

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:05.360
and don’t also include the
100,000 deaths from diabetes

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:08.080
or the 400,000 deaths from heart disease.

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:11.360
So, in actuality, we may be
talking a half million deaths

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:13.680
attributable to processed meat.

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:15.840
And it’s not just
colon and rectal cancer.

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:19.840
If you look at the science since
the IARC decision was published,

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:21.920
processed meat may also
increase the risk of

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:25.920
prostate cancer, breast cancer,
and pancreatic cancer.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:28.560
Unfortunately, despite growing
public health concerns

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:32.000
about processed meat consumption,
there have been no changes

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:34.560
in the amount of processed
meat consumed by U.S. adults

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:36.400
over the last 18 years.

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:37.840
Of course, it would
have helped if the last

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:40.320
Dietary Guidelines for Americans
had happened to mention

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:43.040
that processed meat was a carcinogen.

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:46.080
An explicit and science-based
statement on processed meat

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:49.040
in the next Dietary Guidelines
would certainly help.

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:53.600
But the scientific committee
made no such recommendation.

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:57.600
Sadly, even those diagnosed with
colorectal cancer hardly improve

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:01.440
their overall lifestyle after
diagnosis, though that may be

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:04.800
because “70 percent of cancer
patients had never received

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:06.720
nutrition advice from
their [medical] providers

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:10.800
during or after treatment.”
That just blows me away.

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:13.680
“Despite the continued
obfuscation of the issue

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:17.680
by the meat industry—they learned
well from the tobacco merchants—

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:21.520
meat should continue to be
a focus of public health action.”

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:24.240
New York City is leading
the way, passing legislation

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:26.880
to ban processed meats
from school meals.

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:30.560
What a concept, not giving
our kids carcinogens.

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:32.400
Meanwhile, the processed
meat industry

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:34.480
is trying to reformulate its products.

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:36.800
It’s kind of like in
the pharmaceutical area,

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:38.880
where you try to mitigate
the potential adverse effects

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:41.760
of one drug by prescribing
an additional drug.

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:44.640
Like you could add fiber to
hot dogs or something to try

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:48.080
to counterbalance the risk,
potentially reducing the cancer load

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:50.720
by changing how it’s
processed rather than by

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:53.389
banning processed meat altogether.

